Since we have $\sin^2(\theta)$ in our expression, let's rewrite it using the Pythagorean identity, $\sin^2(\theta)+\cos^2(\theta)=1$:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{1-\cos(\theta)}{2\sin^2(\theta)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1-\cos(\theta)}{2(1-\cos^2(\theta))}\qquad\gray{\text{The Pythagorean identity}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1-\cos(\theta)}{2(1+\cos(\theta))(1-\cos(\theta))}\qquad\gray{\text{Diff. of squares}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{1-\cos(\theta)}}{2(1+\cos(\theta))\cancel{(1-\cos(\theta))}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{2(1+\cos(\theta))}\text{, for }\theta\neq \{...,-4\pi, -2\pi, 0, 2\pi, 4\pi, ...\}
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $\theta $-values (in their domains) except for $2k\pi$ for any integer $k$, and specifically $0$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
